// DF-225e (ruled Aug 20): `sawc/std/` is NOT on a BARE import's search path.
// Only a `std.`-prefixed import reaches std sources, which is design 150's
// uniform model — `import std.data` names the std module, `import data` names
// yours. So a bare `import data` with no user module of that name is the
// ordinary "module not found", reported once.
//
// It used to resolve into `sawc/std/data.saw` behind the reader's back and
// compile that file a SECOND time under the path `data`, beside the real
// `import std.data` above it — so what the reader got was a pile of "defined
// multiple times" errors about std's own internals (`DataBuf` is defined
// multiple times, `make` is already defined for struct `DataBuf`, two
// UnsafeSend/UnsafeSync assertions), pointing INTO std, about a collision the
// program never wrote. The ERROR-ABSENT line is the half that pins the fix:
// the cascade is what has to be gone.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: module `data` not found
// EXPECT-ERROR-ABSENT: defined multiple times

import std.data
import data

func main() {
    var d = data.Data()
    d.push(1)
    print("{d.len()}")
}
